From f7315b14a52f8c0caf117c0165fc7318c546ed59 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 20:44:20 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09105/11362] Say WHY a gated field read None: an absent field
 and a null field need different fixes

REQ-OPS-GATE-NULL-DIAGNOSTIC-6266. Operator asked to deal with the missing
required artifact fields. Tracing them to a cause found one artifact and one
uninformative message.

THE CASE. Milestone .539 cascade-blocked four tasks on
`exp6228-supervised-three-family-runtime-endurance`. All four recorded the same
line: `actual=None == expected=1`. That is true and useless. It cannot say whether
the upstream forgot the field, wrote it as null, or produced nothing at all, and
the three have different fixes. So nobody acted on it, and the recurring-blocker
ledger counted four more anonymous `blocked_gate_check_failed`.

What actually happened was the worst of the three. exp6228's artifact carries
every field the gates name, spelled correctly, with every value `null` --
`honest_verdict` included. A skeleton, not a result. `data.get(field)` returns
None for a missing key and for a key written null, so a check that asks "is the
key present?" sees a complete artifact.

SCOPE, measured before writing anything: 1 artifact in the last 142, and the live
.552 roadmap is clean -- all 12 gates name a field their upstream actually
promises. So this is not systemic. It is one event that cost four tasks and went
undiagnosed for ten milestones because the message did not carry the fact.

THE FIX is diagnosis, not enforcement. `_diagnose_missing_field` splits the None
case in two:

  null     "upstream wrote 'X' as null", plus the count and names of the other
           required-looking fields that are also null. That group is the tell
           that the upstream emitted a template, which is what makes re-running
           the DOWNSTREAM task pointless -- so the message says to re-run the
           upstream instead.
  absent   "upstream artifact has NO field 'X'; it did not honour its own
           REQUIRED ARTIFACT FIELDS, or this gate names a field nobody promised",
           plus the closest keys it did write. The usual cause is spelling drift
           between the gate and the upstream's field list, and naming the near
           miss turns a cascade into a one-word fix.

It never changes whether a gate passes. A guard that alters a verdict while
explaining it is a new bug class, and there is a test asserting a satisfied gate
still passes with no diagnosis attached.

Verified against the real .539 artifact, not a fixture: the same gate that
produced `actual=None == expected=1` now names the null field, lists
`honest_verdict` and three sibling nulls, and says to re-run exp6228.

Three tests, proven to bite by mutation -- collapsing the absent/null branch turns
the .539 test red.

